            body: [
                'en' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>Logistics software connects every handover to cost and service</h2><p>A shipment may pass through booking, collection, consolidation, warehouse, route stages, agents, customs or other authorized processes and final delivery. A controlled logistics platform keeps the cargo, responsible party, documents, status, charges, expenses and customer account connected throughout that journey.</p><p>Fida Technologies can assess systems for cargo companies, transport operators, freight forwarders, distributors, delivery networks and organizations managing their own fleet. The scope must reflect the actual service model rather than combine unrelated workflows into one generic tracking screen.</p>
<h2>Orders, bookings and shipment identity</h2><ul><li>Customer order or booking with origin, destination and service</li><li>Sender, receiver, responsible parties and approved contacts</li><li>Packages, weight, volume, commodity and handling attributes</li><li>Shipment, consignment, load or trip identifiers</li><li>Charges, service terms and required supporting documents</li></ul><p>Identifiers and labels should remain stable across handovers. Duplicate protection, correction authority and document ownership need to be defined before integrations are built.</p>
<h2>Planning routes, loads and capacity</h2><p>Planning may consider delivery commitments, routes, stops, vehicle or carrier capacity, cargo compatibility, driver availability, warehouse cut-offs, transit constraints and approved cost rules. The system can prepare a load or trip plan for review and dispatch.</p><p>Optimization depends on reliable constraints and data. Software should not promise an ideal route when road, border, security, weather, vehicle or partner information is unavailable or outside the system.</p>
<h2>Dispatch, milestones and proof of delivery</h2><p>Execution can record assignment, departure, arrival, pickup, handover, delay, exception, delivery attempt, acceptance and closure. Time, location, responsible user, notes, photographs, signatures or documents may be captured when permitted and operationally practical.</p><ul><li>Planned and actual milestone history</li><li>Exception reason, owner and resolution status</li><li>Customer notifications through approved channels</li><li>Proof of collection or delivery with controlled access</li><li>Claims, damage, shortage or return workflows where required</li></ul>
<h2>Fleet, drivers and maintenance</h2><p>Fleet operators may need vehicle, trailer, driver, assignment, documents, fuel, mileage, tires, maintenance, breakdown and operating-cost records. Expiry reminders can support management of approved vehicle and driver documents, while responsible teams remain accountable for physical checks and legal compliance.</p><p>Organizations using external carriers may instead need rate agreements, capacity requests, tendering, acceptance, performance, documents, freight bills and settlement.</p>
<h2>Warehousing and cross-dock operations</h2><p>When included, logistics warehouses may manage expected receipts, receiving, put-away, locations, inventory status, picking, packing, staging, loading, transfers, cycle counts and dispatch. Cross-dock operations need visibility from inbound receipt to outbound allocation without losing the shipment relationship.</p><p>Warehouse ownership, customer stock, batch or serial needs, damaged goods, quarantine and value-added services should be defined by contract and operating model.</p>
<h2>Freight costing, billing and settlement</h2><p>Approved rates may use service, route, weight, volume, package, vehicle, zone, handling, storage or other agreed inputs. The system can compare expected and actual charges, prepare customer billing, record collections and reconcile carrier, driver or agent expenses and settlements.</p><ul><li>Shipment revenue and approved additional charges</li><li>Fuel, toll, handling, warehouse, carrier and agent costs</li><li>Advances and expense accountability</li><li>Carrier invoice and freight-bill reconciliation</li><li>Customer, route, vehicle, trip or shipment margin views</li></ul><p>Finance must approve accounting, tax, currency and revenue-recognition treatment.</p>
<h2>Documents, integrations and customer visibility</h2><p>The platform may organize manifests, waybills, invoices, packing lists, permits, customs documents and delivery evidence according to the approved process. Customs, trade and transport requirements vary; the responsible organization and qualified advisers must confirm current document and compliance rules.</p><p>Approved APIs may connect customer portals, mobile applications, GPS or telematics providers, warehouse systems, ERP, accounting, payment and partner platforms. Each integration needs identifiers, permissions, validation, error handling, monitoring and reconciliation.</p>
<h2>Roles, security and operational continuity</h2><p>Access can be separated for booking, dispatch, warehouse, driver coordination, agents, customer service, finance, management and administration. Sensitive prices, customer documents, locations, exports and financial adjustments require explicit permissions and audit history.</p><p>Deployment should consider offices, warehouses, vehicles, connectivity, mobile devices, offline or delayed updates, backup, recovery and support. The organization needs a clear procedure for continuing and reconciling work during network or system interruption.</p>
<h2>Dashboards and exception management</h2><p>Useful views may cover unassigned orders, capacity, pickups due, shipments in transit, delayed milestones, exceptions, proof pending, warehouse backlog, vehicle availability, delivery performance, unbilled shipments, receivables and cost variance. Each indicator needs an agreed definition and source.</p>
<h2>Implementation and logistics UAT</h2><ol><li><strong>Discovery:</strong> map services, orders, shipment stages, partners, fleet, warehouses, documents and finance.</li><li><strong>Tracking design:</strong> agree identifiers, statuses, milestones, exceptions, ownership and notifications.</li><li><strong>Phased delivery:</strong> validate representative domestic or cross-border journeys before expansion.</li><li><strong>Migration:</strong> clean customers, routes, vehicles, partners, rates, open shipments, balances and documents.</li><li><strong>QA and UAT:</strong> test partial loads, handovers, delays, damage, returns, billing, settlement, permissions and interruptions.</li><li><strong>Training and launch:</strong> prepare office, warehouse, dispatch and management roles with support and SLA procedures.</li></ol>
<h2>Discuss a transport or logistics platform</h2><p>Share the service types, locations, routes, shipment volumes, fleet or carrier model, warehouses, documents, pricing rules, existing systems, integrations and reporting priorities. Fida can then prepare a scope-based TMS, logistics ERP, fleet, warehouse or integration proposal.</p>
